CLAUDE-SONNET IS OUT, on the clause the spec called "the case that makes a naive implementation
wrong". `cited_line` must accept a number only in a `path:line` position. All three handle the
six shapes the spec listed. On two shapes I added beyond them:

    "see section 2: 300"    codex-luna None   glm None   claude-sonnet Some(300)
    "EXPECT: 45 minutes"    codex-luna None   glm None   claude-sonnet Some(45)

claude-sonnet takes the digits after the FIRST colon with no path validation. `EXPECT:` is a line
in literally every critique this harness writes, so a malformed WHERE would be read as a citation
of line 45 and would corrupt the coverage judgement in the safe-looking direction -- more
citations, shallower apparent reading. It passed every example the spec gave and failed the rule
the spec stated, which is the distinction between testing the letter and the intent.

Between the two survivors, thin as usual: both correct on all eight probes, both call
critic_coverage once, and both pin a test that promotion is unchanged
(`coverage_does_not_change_promotion_results`, `coverage_never_changes_what_is_promoted`).
glm-53-flash has 36 tests to 28, validates the path with a named `looks_like_path` helper, and
handles `path:line:column` by popping segments rather than by position. It is also the free arm.

The only critique, codex-luna on glm-53-flash: NO MATERIAL DEFECTS, plus a real judgement that
`run_cmd` loads claims and reads subjects twice -- once through `critic_coverage`, again through
`coverage_rows` to recover the subject name for the report -- which is read-only but leaves a
window where the map and the printed line could disagree. Worth fixing, not worth blocking.

